  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Conservation Works Activity</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">(Liberia):  FY 2022 biodiversity programming will focus on developing conservation enterprises and managing protected areas (PA) and proposed protected areas (PPA) to promote conservation of the Upper Guinean Forest in Liberia through a cross-cutting Once Health approach. FY 2022 funds will continue to support the Conservation Works Activity as the Mission&apos;s flagship biodiversity activity.  The purpose of this activity is to conserve threatened and endangered species by expanding protected areas and improving economic conditions for Liberians that live near protected areas. This activity utilizes a One Health approach to support a sustainable balance of animal, human and ecosystem health. The core goals of this activity will be the protection of contiguous tracts of forest land which are home to threatened and endangered species and the improvement of livelihoods for rural communities. The core objectives of the activity include: 1) harmonize the establishment process of protected and conserved areas; 2) strengthen the management of protected and conserved areas; 3) develop, establish, and facilitate sustainable livelihoods; 4) develop sustainable tourism infrastructure and capacity; and 5) mainstream protected and conserved areas and conservation-compatible investment across Liberia. .Using FY 2022 funds, USAID will support legal recognition of new protected areas, improved management and governance capacity at the national and community level, development of community livelihoods, growth of ecotourism, and capacity development for Liberia&apos;s Wildlife Crime Task Force, Forestry Development Authority, Ministry of Justice, and Liberia National Police. The community-centered interventions will help shift dependence away from extractive use of forest resources and toward more sustainable and resilient livelihood opportunities. Ecotourism efforts will be focused at the national level and on site-specific interventions at potential ecotourism sites.</narrative>
  </description>
